\chapter{Wayland and the Ownership of a Frame}
\label{ch:wayland}
\index{Wayland}\index{GPU}\index{DRM/KMS}\index{buffers!graphics}

\section{A different protocol boundary}

Wayland, begun by Kristian Høgsberg in 2008, defines communication between
clients and a compositor. The project's own overview emphasizes input
handling and buffer management. Clients render their content; the
compositor receives buffers, arranges surfaces, routes input, and controls
presentation. Wayland is a protocol and supporting libraries, not one
mandatory desktop implementation \parencite{wayland}.

That last distinction matters in practical discussion. GNOME's Mutter,
KDE's KWin, and compositors built with libraries such as wlroots can
implement Wayland while differing in policy, extensions, integration,
and behavior. A problem observed on one compositor is not automatically
a property of every Wayland environment.

The compositor can run on the hardware, inside another window system,
or as a client of another compositor. The protocol does not require that
every compositor directly owns a physical monitor. This flexibility also
makes testing and nested sessions possible without redefining the basic
client relationship.

The new boundary reflects work already done by modern clients and
compositors. It removes the need to treat historical server-side drawing
operations as the center of the architecture. It does not remove the
underlying difficulty of graphics hardware, input devices, or desktop
integration.

\section{Follow a frame from application to display}

An application decides that part of its content has changed.
Its toolkit and rendering libraries produce pixels in a buffer.
The client associates that content with a surface and commits an update
through the Wayland protocol. The compositor then considers the surface
alongside other surfaces and the current output configuration.

If composition is needed, the compositor combines content into an output
image, often using the GPU. If conditions permit, it may present a client
buffer more directly. Kernel modesetting facilities control display
configuration and presentation at the hardware boundary.
The exact path depends on drivers, formats, scaling, monitors, and policy.

The frame therefore passes through several agreements:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{p{0.24\textwidth}p{0.62\textwidth}}
\toprule
Participant & Principal responsibility \\
\midrule
Client and toolkit & Content, layout, rendering, application interaction \\
Compositor & Surface arrangement, input routing, presentation policy \\
Userspace driver & Rendering API implementation and device commands \\
Kernel graphics driver & Hardware resource management and display control \\
Display hardware & Scanout and physical output \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

This is a responsibility map, not a claim that each row is a separate
process or that every frame takes an identical route. The map prevents
one common confusion: a rendering API, display protocol, and graphics
driver solve related but different problems.

\section{A buffer is more than a pointer}
\index{dma-buf}\index{synchronization!graphics}

A software-rendered client can use shared memory for pixel storage.
Accelerated rendering commonly uses mechanisms that let participants
refer to graphics buffers without copying every pixel through the
protocol connection. Linux dma-buf is important in sharing such resources
across components.

Sharing requires agreement on dimensions, pixel format, layout modifiers,
and access. A buffer whose bytes are meaningful to one driver may not be
usable by another participant without a compatible import path.
A handle transfers a reference to storage; it does not automatically
transfer a complete understanding of its representation.

Timing is equally important. Rendering may still be in progress when the
client submits the buffer. The compositor must know when it is safe to
read, and the client must know when storage can be reused.
Implicit and explicit synchronization provide different ways to express
these dependencies. Support varies with the complete driver and compositor
stack, not merely with the name of the protocol.

This is the graphics version of the lifetime problems already encountered
in files and processes. Reusing a buffer too early can corrupt a frame;
retaining it forever leaks resources. A smooth desktop depends on these
agreements being maintained many times per second.

\section{Security changes useful habits}

Wayland's core model does not grant an ordinary client a general right to
inspect every other surface or capture every keystroke.
Input is routed by the compositor, and access to wider desktop information
requires additional mechanisms and policy. This supports stronger
separation among applications than the traditional shared-trust X model.

The change affects legitimate software as well as attackers.
Screen capture, remote control, global shortcuts, automation, input
methods, and accessibility tools need carefully designed interfaces.
A feature that once worked by observing the whole display cannot simply
reuse that route when the route is deliberately absent.

Desktop portals and services such as PipeWire provide important pieces
of modern Linux capture and sharing workflows. A portal can mediate a
request and obtain user consent; a media service can carry selected
streams. The details depend on desktop and portal implementation.
Saying ``Wayland supports screen sharing'' is less informative than naming
the complete path and the versions that supply it.

Security is therefore an integration project, not only a restriction.
If an architecture removes a broad authority, it needs usable replacements
for legitimate tasks. Otherwise users and developers will seek workarounds
that restore the old authority through less visible channels.

\section{Extensions carry the rest of the desktop}

A minimal core protocol cannot describe every need of a complete desktop.
Extensions cover matters such as toplevel window roles, decorations,
output information, presentation timing, and other specialized behavior.
Some protocols are widely standardized; others are experimental or tied
to particular compositor communities.

This creates a familiar balance. Keeping the core small makes it easier
to understand and preserve. Leaving too much outside a common agreement
can produce inconsistent capabilities across environments.
Protocol development consequently requires coordination among toolkits,
compositors, application authors, and sometimes driver developers.

Fractional scaling illustrates the issue. A logical application coordinate
and a physical output pixel need not have a one-to-one relationship.
Applications, toolkits, and compositors must agree on buffer scale,
resampling, and appropriate sizes. Mixed-density monitors make the problem
more demanding. A feature cannot be reduced to a single checkbox in the
core protocol.

Color management, high-dynamic-range presentation, and variable refresh
introduce further contracts. Their development changes rapidly, so this
book avoids a blanket claim that every Wayland desktop offers the same
capabilities. The stable historical point is that presentation policy
has moved toward a compositor-centered architecture while detailed
agreements continue to evolve.

\section{Xwayland makes migration incremental}
\index{Xwayland}

A display transition cannot require every application to be rewritten at
once. Xwayland runs an X server as a Wayland client, allowing many existing
X applications to appear within a Wayland session. It preserves the older
application protocol while adapting presentation to the newer environment.

Compatibility has limits. An application relying on global X behavior
may not receive equivalent authority over the whole Wayland desktop.
Input, scaling, capture, and window-management details can differ.
The compatibility layer is a bridge between contracts with different
assumptions, not a promise that those assumptions have become identical.

The arrangement resembles other successful transitions in Unix history.
A stable interface protects existing software while a lower layer changes.
The bridge lets institutions migrate in stages, but it also adds a
component whose bugs and performance must be understood.
Compatibility reduces the cost of change without making change costless.

\section{A new display system, the same engineering questions}

Wayland is often discussed as a contest between desktop preferences.
The more useful technical questions concern ownership and authority.
Who renders? Who may inspect a surface? Who routes a key?
Who owns a buffer at a given instant? Which component can prove the GPU
has finished writing it?

Those questions connect the graphics stack to the rest of the book.
A buffer needs a lifetime, a protocol needs versioning, an extension needs
adoption, and a security boundary needs a practical user experience.
The replacement of one display protocol is therefore both a technical
and an institutional history.

Unix graphics has moved from printing characters to composing GPU-rendered
surfaces without reaching a final arrangement. Each stage made a different
set of assumptions economical. The enduring work is to identify those
assumptions clearly enough that the next stage can change them.
